why 3 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano (E–C♯) and tenor (B–C♯) both reach C♯, the third, and the tenor moves on to D at once. A doubling for one eighth.
why 7 1/4 PROGRESSION The tune climbs D–E–F♯ and the bass falls B–A–G–F♯ in contrary motion. The chords over this line are passing ones, not a step backward.
why 9 3/4 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to C♯, above where the tenor was, while the tenor leaps up to E. Both move up together, so the order stays clear.
why 9 3/4 RANGE The bass leaps up to C♯ for the iii chord and falls back to F♯ right away. A short high point.
why 11 1/8 UNRESOLVED_NCT The bass passes A–F♯–E–D, skipping over G♯. The F♯ is a passing note in that descent; Bach skips a step to reach the IV chord on the beat.
